      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Religious conservatives generally argue against physician-assisted dying, often based on the belief in the inalienable sanctity of human life as a divine gift. They frequently emphasize the inherent value of all life and the potential for the practice to lead to the \"slippery slope\" of devaluing life in general.\n\nIn contrast, religious liberals often support the right to choose, arguing for personal autonomy and the compassionate care of the terminally ill in good faith. This perspective often focuses on the duty to alleviate suffering and support the individual's deeply held beliefs about the end of life.",
